        <div class="faq" id="faq">
            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
            <h3>Q1: Can one gasket material work for all aerosol formulations?</h3>
            <p>No. Elastomer compatibility depends on formulation chemistry, propellant type, and temperature exposure. Universal gasket solutions do not exist.</p>
            <h3>Q2: Why do some aerosols leak only after months of storage?</h3>
            <p>Chemical diffusion and elastomer degradation are time-dependent processes. Failures often occur after gradual swelling or compression set develops.</p>
            <h3>Q3: Is gasket failure always visible during production testing?</h3>
            <p>No. Many failures emerge only after accelerated aging or real-world storage conditions.</p>
            <h3>Q4: Does higher-cost gasket material always mean better performance?</h3>
            <p>Not necessarily. The correct material is defined by compatibility, not price. Premium elastomers are justified only when chemically required.</p>
